{"name":"Neck bridges / wrestler bridges","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Throgs_Neck_Bridge_from_the_air.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Throgs_Neck_Bridge_from_the_air.jpg","caption":"Throgs Neck Bridge from the air","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A neck bridge is an arch where the head or neck helps support the body, drawn from wrestling and grappling sports. Looksmaxxers borrow it to thicken the neck and get a stronger, more powerful look.","The position loads the neck and back muscles with bodyweight. It is documented in wrestling, judo and mixed martial arts, and general bridge references describe it as mainly for lower back and gluteal strength. No studies have tested it for neck thickness."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People practise bridges as a bodyweight exercise, often borrowing progressions from wrestling training, as part of a routine aimed at neck size."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No injury data for cosmetic neck bridging were found, and risks are not documented in the available sources."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Throgs_Neck_Bridge_from_the_air.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Throgs_Neck_Bridge_from_the_air.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Throgs Neck Bridge from the air","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","creator":"Photo originally by Sfoskett Cropped and exposure adjusted by User:Sam","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Throgs_Neck_Bridge_from_the_air.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Aerial_View_of_the_Throgs_Neck_Bridg