ge_(exercise)"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Physique","chain":["Bodyweight load on neck","Neck muscles thicken","Thicker, more powerful neck"],"effect":"indirect","text":"Looksmaxxers borrow the wrestler's bridge from combat sports to load the neck with bodyweight and thicken it for a stronger, more masculine look. The exercise is documented in wrestling, judo and MMA, mainly for back and glute strength."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":null,"lead":["A neck bridge, or wrestler's bridge, is a bodyweight position in which a person arches the back with weight supported on the feet and the head or neck, used in wrestling and combat sports and discussed in looksmaxxing circles as a way to thicken the neck. A general reference on the bridge exercise describes it as used in sporting and self-defense settings such as wrestling, judo and mixed martial arts, and as intended mainly for lower back and gluteal strength [1].","No studies of neck bridges for cosmetic neck thickness were found. Searches of the medical literature return mostly unrelated material, such as 'neck-bridge' devices used in aneurysm surgery, which are unconnected to the exercise."],"infobox":[["Type","Bodyweight exercise / combat-sport drill"],["Used in","Wrestling, judo, MMA"],["Claimed appearance benefit","Thicker neck"],["Clinical evidence","None found for appearance"]],"sections":[{"h":"Description","paras":["Bridge exercises generally involve arching the body so that only some points touch the ground; variations include single-leg and one-arm forms, which load the hamstrings, glutes and spinal erectors [1]. Wrestling and other grappling sports use bridging to escape pins, which is why the neck-supported version is associated with those sports [1]."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks","paras":["The general bridge article does not discuss risks of neck-loaded bridging [1]. No clinical data on injury rates for cosmetic neck bridging were found."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found. Medical literature on 'neck-bridge' devices concerns aneurysm treatment and is unrelated to the exercise."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[],"refs":[{"title":"Bridge (exercise)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridge_(exercise)","source":"Wikipedia","year":2025,"type":"encyclopedia"}],"studies":[],"reddit":[],"forums":[{"title":"Rebirth of the Juggernaut: Brute Force and Ignorance (Part 1)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/rebirth-of-the-juggernaut-brute-force-and-ignorance-part-1/212755","site":"T Nation · 10000 posts · 2015"},{"title":"More Trouble Than I Am Worth: Chaos Is The Plan (T3hPwnisher Log)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/more-trouble-than-i-am-worth-chaos-is-the-plan-t3hpwnisher-log/276232","site":"T Nation · 8674 posts · 2021"},{"title":"The Tactical Life","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/the-tactical-life/232312","site":"T Nation · 5251 posts · 2017"},{"title":"Titan Tim Tackling his Twenties","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/titan-tim-tackling-his-twenties/254087","site":"T Nation · 4707 posts · 2019"},{"title":"Atlas13 Burning the Boats","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/atlas13-burning-the-boats/259312","site":"T Nation · 3714 posts · 2019"},{"title":"A New Animal: JDM135 for 1-2-3-4 plates","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/a-new-animal-jdm135-for-1-2-3-4-plates/263785","site":"T Nation · 2688 posts · 2020"}]}